1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,739
You have two evenings.

2
00:00:02,189 --> 00:00:03,928
A campaign is twenty.

3
00:00:05,728 --> 00:00:09,207
Today you'll put all twenty on one page,

4
00:00:09,657 --> 00:00:17,048
and you'll lay the first act down in the game, from its first lead to its last.

5
00:00:18,848 --> 00:00:23,196
And then you'll count what it's going to cost you.

6
00:00:24,396 --> 00:00:28,743
Twenty evenings is too many to hold in your head,

7
00:00:29,193 --> 00:00:31,367
but five of them isn't.

8
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:35,341
So a campaign is acts,

9
00:00:35,791 --> 00:00:40,139
and an act is about five evenings with one question.

10
00:00:41,939 --> 00:00:44,113
What happened to the boats?

11
00:00:44,563 --> 00:00:47,172
Who took the people off them?

12
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:49,811
Where did they go?

13
00:00:50,261 --> 00:00:53,304
And do they want to be found?

14
00:00:55,104 --> 00:00:58,583
Notice how each answer is the next question.

15
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:02,961
And notice the last row is one line.

16
00:01:03,861 --> 00:01:09,078
You don't know yet what your crew will have done by then.

17
00:01:10,278 --> 00:01:14,626
Now the act you're about to build, evening by evening.

18
00:01:16,426 --> 00:01:19,904
Look at the place column before anything else.

19
00:01:20,804 --> 00:01:26,457
Two of these five evenings go somewhere you built in the last class.

20
00:01:28,257 --> 00:01:29,996
And that's quite deliberate.

21
00:01:30,896 --> 00:01:34,374
A campaign that only goes to new places

22
00:01:34,824 --> 00:01:37,433
teaches the crew that nowhere matters.

23
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:40,072
So send them back.

24
00:01:41,272 --> 00:01:43,880
You've written two evenings in full.

25
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:46,939
The other three start as stubs.

26
00:01:48,739 --> 00:01:53,087
A stub is an open with nothing behind it yet.

27
00:01:53,987 --> 00:01:57,030
One lead, that shows the next lead.

28
00:01:58,830 --> 00:02:04,917
With three of them, the act can be walked from end to end tonight.

29
00:02:05,817 --> 00:02:11,035
And lint can see that every evening's joined to the one before.

30
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:17,183
A stub is a real evening, only a short one.

31
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:20,691
If the crew outruns your writing,

32
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:25,489
they get a thin evening, and not a dead end.

33
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:30,602
The act ends at home, with a big payment,

34
00:02:31,052 --> 00:02:34,530
and a text that answers the act's question.

35
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:39,809
It's a tentpole, so it gets a page.

36
00:02:40,709 --> 00:02:42,883
At the top, a face:

37
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:45,941
the registrar who keeps the Count.

38
00:02:46,841 --> 00:02:49,450
I copy the face string exactly,

39
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,509
because one cut short still draws,

40
00:02:52,959 --> 00:02:54,698
as half a face.

41
00:02:56,498 --> 00:02:58,237
Then two short paragraphs,

42
00:02:58,687 --> 00:03:03,035
and the whole count in one table, all five boats.

43
00:03:04,835 --> 00:03:07,443
And it has no next step.

44
00:03:08,343 --> 00:03:10,083
Act two isn't written,

45
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:14,446
so no new lead appears, and that's the truth.

46
00:03:16,246 --> 00:03:20,593
When you have evening six, you join it on here.

47
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:27,146
The capstone shows how to do that for a crew that's already finished.

48
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:31,824
A word about winning, because I measured it.

49
00:03:33,624 --> 00:03:39,276
A step with a win line ends the game the moment it's done.

50
00:03:40,176 --> 00:03:43,220
The save keeps that step as done.

51
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,193
The crew can continue afterward,

52
00:03:47,643 --> 00:03:50,252
and the game simply goes on.

53
00:03:51,152 --> 00:03:55,935
But that win can never happen again, because it's been spent.

54
00:03:57,735 --> 00:03:59,909
So a campaign has one,

55
00:04:00,359 --> 00:04:03,837
on the last step of the last evening,

56
00:04:04,287 --> 00:04:06,461
and you write it last.

57
00:04:07,661 --> 00:04:09,835
Now it's time to count.

58
00:04:10,735 --> 00:04:14,648
The printed bible does some of it for you.

59
00:04:16,448 --> 00:04:22,100
Near the top is a block that says how many records you have,

60
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:26,028
and how many beats are on the spine.

61
00:04:27,828 --> 00:04:30,437
Then count one evening by hand.

62
00:04:30,887 --> 00:04:34,365
Four records. About ten sentences the crew reads.

63
00:04:36,165 --> 00:04:39,643
Seventeen ordinary evenings, three tentpoles, four act endings.

64
00:04:40,543 --> 00:04:42,283
That's about ninety records,

65
00:04:42,733 --> 00:04:44,472
and you've written thirteen.

66
00:04:46,272 --> 00:04:52,793
Now write down how long evening two took you to write, and multiply it up.

67
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:57,172
Do that before you promise anyone a campaign.

68
00:04:58,372 --> 00:05:01,415
Here's the good news in that number.

69
00:05:03,215 --> 00:05:06,259
The spine is only half an evening.

70
00:05:07,159 --> 00:05:11,072
The other half is the universe you already built.

71
00:05:12,872 --> 00:05:15,480
Jobs come round again every week.

72
00:05:15,930 --> 00:05:18,104
So do hails, and trade.

73
00:05:19,004 --> 00:05:23,352
So you're not writing twenty evenings of things to do.

74
00:05:24,252 --> 00:05:27,730
You're writing twenty reasons to go out there.

75
00:05:28,930 --> 00:05:31,974
Lint's clean, so let's walk the act.

76
00:05:32,874 --> 00:05:35,917
I'm not playing. I'm checking the joins.

77
00:05:37,717 --> 00:05:41,196
Three of five, and four appears at once.

78
00:05:42,096 --> 00:05:44,270
Four, and there's the Petrel.

79
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,763
Five, and we're in the breaking yard.

80
00:05:49,563 --> 00:05:51,302
And then we're home.

81
00:05:52,202 --> 00:05:53,941
That's a thousand credits,

82
00:05:54,391 --> 00:05:57,870
and every line of the campaign is done.

83
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:05,322
And on the way, two old leads from the last class finished themselves,

84
00:06:05,772 --> 00:06:08,815
because the spine goes through those systems.

85
00:06:10,015 --> 00:06:13,928
So you have four acts, one of them walkable,

86
00:06:14,378 --> 00:06:16,117
and an honest number.

87
00:06:17,917 --> 00:06:20,091
If that number's too big,

88
00:06:20,541 --> 00:06:24,454
the answer is fewer evenings, and not thinner ones.

89
00:06:25,354 --> 00:06:29,702
Next time, we give the acts something to turn on.
